f (tokens[k].kind == REMIMU_KIND_CLOSE) \\\n    { \\\n        q_group_state[tokens[k].mask[0]] = rewind_stack[stack_n].group_state; \\\n        q_group_stack[tokens[k].mask[0]] = rewind_stack[stack_n].prev; \\\n    } \\\n    k -= 1; \\\n}\n```\n\nSo REWIND_OR_ABORT decrements stack_n once initially, then skips dummies. The state read is the new top. If that state is a CLOSE, `q_group_stack[m0]` is restored to its prev. The state being read is NOT consumed in the C sense — it stays in the array.\n\nIn my Rust translation, I'm popping the data. So the state at the new top (in C) is being lost in Rust. Hmm. Let me think about whether this matters.\n\nWait, in C: stack_n=3, decrement to 2, read rewind_stack[2]. After return, stack_n=2 still. The data at rewind_stack[2] is still there. The next save would write at index 2.\n\nIn my Rust: pop returns the entry at index 2. Stack now has length 2. Future push goes to index 2.\n\nHmm yes, but the data is lost. But that data was just used to set i, k, range_min, etc. So that's fine.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: the data at rewind_stack[2] before decrement is preserved by C until overwritten. My Rust loses it. Could `prev` from another entry point to that index? Well, it's the current top, so any `prev` chain would go backward. After popping, `prev` indices into the stack could still point to valid (now lower) indices. They wouldn't point to index 2 (the popped one) unless there's a `prev = 2`. But that would only happen if some saved state (still on stack at index < 2) had `prev = 2`, which would mean we saved AFTER index 2. Impossible since the saves at >2 are above 2 and got popped or have `prev` pointing to something <= 2.\n\nActually wait. Consider: save A at index 0, save B at index 1, save C at index 2 (with prev pointing to B at 1). REWIND_OR_ABORT pops C. Now stack=[A,B]. Then we re-save C (or something else) at index 2. That new save might have prev pointing to some other thing, not necessarily to old C. So we're fine.\n\nBut there's a different issue: let's say save A at index 0, save B at index 1 with prev=0 (pointing to A). Save C at index 2 with prev=1 (pointing to B). Save D at index 3 with prev=2 (pointing to C). REWIND_OR_ABORT pops D, restores via D's data. q_group_stack now contains 2 (D's prev which was C). But stack only has [A,B,C] now. C is at index 2. So q_group_stack=2 correctly points to C. OK.\n\nNow another REWIND_OR_ABORT pops C. q_group_stack restored to 1 (C's prev). Correct - B at index 1.\n\nOK so the data integrity is preserved as long as we pop in a stack-like manner.\n\nNow the index OOB at line 905:\n\n```rust\nif prev != 0 && (rewind_stack[prev as usize].i as u32) > (i as u32) {\n```\n\nThis reads `rewind_stack[prev]`. So `prev` must be < rewind_stack.len(). When is this not satisfied?\n\n`prev = q_group_stack[m0]` is set when save is made. It's the index of the entry. As long as `q_group_stack` is properly maintained, prev should always be < stack_n.\n\nMaybe the issue is when `rewind_stack` got popped beyond what `q_group_stack[m0]` knows. Let me trace through `(ab?)b` on `ab`:\n\nActually let me first run the tests again with the latest fix:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1540,"cache_read_input_tokens":131794,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1540,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e298ebc4-2d7e-4a4f-8879-892213f222f9","uuid":"daffc822-9f5d-41bc-afbc-27343d91beb5"}
